<GuidePage
  title="Directional Fabric Cutting for Quilt Pieces"
  guideSlug="directional-fabric-cutting"
  guideCategory="reference"
  description="Keep one-way prints and grain-sensitive quilt pieces oriented correctly when calculating yardage and laying out cuts."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Direction changes which rotations are legal</h2><p>
      A rectangle may physically fit after a quarter turn but place a one-way
      motif sideways. Mark directional fabric and restrict rotation when
      orientation matters.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>WOF strips are a stronger constraint</h2><p>
      A width-of-fabric strip must span the usable crosswise width. It cannot
      rotate into the lengthwise axis merely because the dimensions fit.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Plan with the restriction enabled</h2><p>
      The <a href="/calculators/fabric-yardage/">yardage calculator</a> exposes rotation
      and direction. The <a href="/fabric-cutting-planner/">project planner</a> also
      supports piece-level orientation overrides.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
